{"context_ids": [], "controls": [{"control_id": "ngram_sparse_grad_off_same_binary", "description": "Identical binary, identical env, NGRAM_SPARSE_GRAD=0, same seed, same GPU pool, interleaved across GPUs within each batch to avoid the cross-GPU contention confound recorded in the campaign.", "kind": "no_intervention", "matching": {"same_checkpoint": true, "same_data_order": true}}], "created_at": "2026-07-30T21:20:00Z", "estimated_cost": {"basis": "20 one-H200 arms at a 300s charged budget plus compile warmup, 5-wide on free GPUs.", "cost_tier": "moderate", "currency_cost": 0.0, "gpu_hours": 1.6667}, "expected_effect": {"direction": "decrease", "estimand": "paired_raw_val_bpb_sparse_on_minus_off_walltime_5min_h200", "latency_steps": 0}, "falsification": {"decision_rule": "PREREGISTERED, fixed before data. (1) TRAP-CHECK on batch 1: if treatment step count is not clearly above control, the mechanism did not fire in this config and the round is killed -- no endpoint is claimed. (2) DECISION metric is the RAW paired delta, because the throughput gain IS the mechanism; the token-law control variate is a contention-nuisance correction and applying it to the treatment's own designed effect would subtract the hypothesis. Adoption requires RAW mean <= -0.002396 at n=10 clean pairs through the standard funnel. (3) SECONDARY: the INTRINSIC delta tests the separate quality-neutrality claim; an intrinsic delta worse than +0.0012 falsifies quality-neutrality even if RAW clears the gate, and must be reported as a partial result rather than a clean adoption. (4) Any arm failing the contention screen (steps >= 1950 primary, median MFU >= 18.5 secondary) is quarantined with the reason recorded, and its PAIR is dropped.", "failure_condition": "RAW mean delta > -0.002396 at n=10, or treatment step count not above control. The specific expected failure mode, given six prior kills: the custom_op boundary blocks inductor epilogue fusion around the n-gram path and the lost fusion costs more than the removed fills.", "minimum_seeds": 10}, "fingerprint": "139486b16fa3def0", "hypothesis_id": "hyp_e6_ngram_sparse_grad_token_law", "intervention": {"intervention_id": "int_ngram_sparse_grad_treatment", "parameters": {"env": {"ATTN_BACKEND": "fa3", "COMPILE_MODE": "max-autotune-no-cudagraphs", "DEVICE_BATCH_SIZE": "72", "MATRIX_LR": "0.04", "NGRAM_SPARSE_GRAD": "1", "STOP_MODE": "time", "TIME_BUDGET": "300", "TOTAL_BATCH_SIZE": "147456", "WINDOW_PATTERN": "TTTL"}}, "version": 1}, "mechanism_ids": ["mech_ngram_dense_embedding_backward_fill_flood"], "notes": "DIRECTION SWITCH, evidence-driven. Papers 026/027 plus E5 give five preregistered zero-cost INTRINSIC interventions -- E1 cond-init -0.000157, E2 Peri-LN -0.000092, E3 GPAS +0.001269, E5 shuffle +0.000129 -- all inside +-1.3e-3 against a 2.396e-3 gate. The intrinsic channel is measured shut in this frame. The throughput channel measures ~1e-2 and is the only channel with headroom, bounded above by the data wall at -0.06*ln(1.45) = -0.022. E6 is the first candidate in many blocks whose PREDICTED effect exceeds the gate before launch.\n\nOUTCOME (2026-07-31, block28): REFUTED at the trap-check, in the opposite direction. 1779 vs 2016 mean steps over 5 clean pairs = +13.37% step time against a predicted -9.3%. The falsification record's named expected failure mode -- custom_op blocking inductor epilogue fusion -- is the one that occurred. The mechanism mech_ngram_dense_embedding_backward_fill_flood is NOT thereby refuted: its discriminating prediction is that the sparse benefit scales with TABLE ROWS, and the prior clean A/B measured -9.3% at NGRAM_TABLE_MULT=256 while this round ran the frame default. A sign flip between multipliers is what fill-flood predicts and what the rival bandwidth account forbids. E7 (2x2 mult {64,256} x sparse {0,1}) tests exactly that interaction and was designed before this data existed.", "observable_predictions": [{"expected_pattern": {"comparison": "treatment_gt_control", "pairs": 10, "point_estimate_pct": 10.2, "sign_pairs_min": 8}, "observable_id": "obs_tok_per_sec_k", "version": 1, "window": {"aggregate": "median", "type": "full_run"}}, {"expected_pattern": {"comparison": "treatment_lt_control", "pairs": 10, "point_estimate_pct": -9.3, "sign_pairs_min": 8}, "observable_id": "obs_step_time_ms", "version": 1, "window": {"aggregate": "median", "type": "full_run"}}], "outcome": {"outcome_id": "out_val_bpb", "version": 1}, "prediction": "Paired over 10 seeds in walltime_5min_h200, NGRAM_SPARSE_GRAD=1 lowers RAW val_bpb by ~0.0058 relative to NGRAM_SPARSE_GRAD=0, mediated entirely by completing ~10.2% more steps. The throughput-corrected INTRINSIC delta is predicted to be null (|delta| < 0.0012), because the mechanism changes scheduling, not learning.", "status": "rejected", "tags": ["e6", "block27", "throughput", "ngram", "sparse_grad", "direction_switch"], "title": "Removing the n-gram dense-backward fill flood buys tokens, and tokens buy bpb", "trigger": {"condition": {"operator": "batch_one_trap_check_step_count_above_control"}, "observable_id": "", "type": "run_start"}, "version": 2}
{"context_ids": ["ctx_five_minute_h200"], "controls": [{"control_id": "ngram_wd_off_same_binary", "description": "Identical binary and env with NGRAM_WD_LAMBDA=0 and DIAGNOSE=1, same seed, interleaved across GPUs within each batch against the cross-GPU contention confound. DIAGNOSE is validation-only and adds ~0% training overhead, so it runs in BOTH arms.", "kind": "no_intervention", "matching": {"same_checkpoint": true, "same_data_order": true}}], "created_at": "2026-08-01T20:10:00Z", "estimated_cost": {"basis": "8 one-H200 arms (4 paired seeds) at a 300s charged budget plus compile warmup, 4-wide.", "cost_tier": "light", "currency_cost": 0.0, "gpu_hours": 0.6667}, "expected_effect": {"direction": "increase", "estimand": "paired_rare_minus_frequent_decile_nats_increase_under_ngram_decay_walltime_5min_h200", "latency_steps": 0}, "falsification": {"decision_rule": "PREREGISTERED, fixed before data. This is a PRECONDITION test whose purpose is to kill an expensive experiment cheaply, so the decision rule is deliberately biased AGAINST proceeding. (1) TRAP-CHECK: both arms must emit DIAGNOSE_FREQ lines for all ten deciles and the treatment must reproduce the ~93% gap closure; if not, the arm did not fire and no verdict is claimed. (2) SEPARABLE if the rare/frequent damage ratio >= 1.5 in at least 4 of 4 pairs -- N2 is then funded. (3) NOT SEPARABLE if the ratio lies in [0.8, 1.25] -- N2 is CLOSED without being run, and the weight-space family closes on evidence rather than on argument. (4) INCONCLUSIVE otherwise; report the third outcome as its own verdict rather than rounding it. (5) val_bpb is NOT the decision metric here and no adoption is possible from this round; the endpoint is recorded only to confirm the known degradation reproduces. (6) The token-law control variate does NOT apply -- this is not a throughput mechanism -- and any arm failing the two-sided contention screen against the stored clean reference is quarantined with its pair dropped.", "failure_condition": "Flat damage across deciles. NAMED EXPECTED FAILURE MODE, and it is the one the prior evidence favours: the v3 campaign's own decile probe found the n-gram CAPACITY benefit was BROAD rather than rare-specific (5.26% relative improvement on frequent tokens vs 4.14% on rare, i.e. flat-to-DECLINING with rarity). If capacity acts broadly, decay probably also acts broadly, and the ratio comes out flat.", "minimum_seeds": 4}, "fingerprint": "4ad51784fdf84af9", "hypothesis_id": "hyp_n2_precondition_decile_separability", "intervention": {"intervention_id": "int_ngram_table_wd", "parameters": {"env": {"ATTN_BACKEND": "fa3", "COMPILE_MODE": "max-autotune-no-cudagraphs", "DEVICE_BATCH_SIZE": "72", "DIAGNOSE": "1", "MATRIX_LR": "0.04", "NGRAM_WD_LAMBDA": "0.1", "STOP_MODE": "time", "TIME_BUDGET": "300", "TOTAL_BATCH_SIZE": "147456", "WINDOW_PATTERN": "TTTL"}}, "version": 1}, "mechanism_ids": ["mech_memoriser_is_the_model"], "notes": "HOW THIS CHAIN WAS DERIVED, which is the point of registering it.\n\nSTEP 1 -- read the experimental feedback, not an idea list. Two registered observations bear on the same question and DISAGREE. evd_obs_ngram_decay_gap_endpoint_decoupling says uniform decay closes 93% of the train/val gap and still degrades the endpoint, which is what motivated mech_memoriser_is_the_model: the memorising rows and the signal rows are the same rows. That mechanism's one surviving intervention (int_ngram_count_conditioned_decay) assumes the two populations ARE separable by visit frequency. But the v3 campaign already probed frequency structure in this architecture and found the capacity benefit BROAD, not rare-specific.\n\nSTEP 2 -- name the disagreement as a measurable quantity. If the populations are frequency-separated, decay's damage concentrates in the rare deciles; if not, it is flat. obs_val_nats_by_freq_decile already exists, is validation-only, and costs ~0% training overhead. The discriminator was in the toolkit before the question was asked.\n\nSTEP 3 -- put it BEFORE the expensive experiment it gates. N2 costs ~1.7 GPU-h and a c* sweep; this precondition costs 0.67 GPU-h and can close N2 outright. Ordering the cheap discriminator first is the whole value of authoring the chain rather than launching the idea.\n\nSTEP 4 -- record the evidence AGAINST before launching. The prior evidence favours the null. This hypothesis is registered expecting to fail, because a cheap expected-failure that closes a family is worth more than an expensive maybe.\n\nSCOPE CAVEAT, stated because it weakens my own case for running it: the v3 decile probe measured capacity ADDED (64x vs 256x) while this measures capacity PENALISED. Those are different contrasts and the transfer is suggestive, not decisive -- which is precisely why the measurement is worth its 0.67 GPU-h instead of being settled by argument.", "observable_predictions": [{"expected_pattern": {"comparison": "treatment_gt_control", "pairs": 4, "rare_over_frequent_damage_ratio_min": 1.5, "sign_pairs_min": 4}, "observable_id": "obs_val_nats_by_freq_decile", "version": 1, "window": {"aggregate": "mean", "type": "end_of_run"}}, {"expected_pattern": {"comparison": "treatment_lt_control", "pairs": 4, "point_estimate_pct": -93.0, "sign_pairs_min": 4}, "observable_id": "obs_train_val_gap", "version": 1, "window": {"aggregate": "final", "type": "full_run"}}], "outcome": {"outcome_id": "out_val_bpb", "version": 1}, "prediction": "Paired over 4 seeds, turning on uniform n-gram table decay raises validation nats in the three RAREST frequency deciles by at least 1.5x as much as in the three most FREQUENT deciles. The gap closure (~93%) reproduces, confirming the arm fired. If the damage ratio is instead flat, the memorising rows and the statistic-carrying rows are NOT separated by token frequency, and count-conditioned decay -- the only surviving member of the weight-space family -- is attacking a population that does not exist as a distinguishable set.", "status": "proposed", "tags": ["n2", "precondition", "ngram", "regularisation", "decile_probe", "expected_null", "kills_a_family_cheaply"], "title": "Uniform n-gram decay damages rare-frequency deciles disproportionately -- or count-conditioned decay cannot work and must not be funded", "trigger": {"condition": {"operator": "diagnose_freq_lines_present_in_both_arms"}, "observable_id": "", "type": "run_start"}, "version": 1}
